# Rust Backend Conventions

## Architecture Boundaries

- Treat the backend as an offchain coordination service only (proposal creation, signature collection, and lifecycle tracking)
- Do not re-implement or override canonical protocol validity rules enforced by the ASM/SPS-65 logic
- Allow only hygiene checks server-side (malformed input, duplicate signatures, structural consistency)
- Keep handlers thin and place domain logic in dedicated modules/services
- Keep role/authority concerns isolated (Alpen Admin, Strata Admin, Sequencer Manager, Security Council, Payout Admin)

## API and Auth

- Build HTTP APIs with Axum patterns: typed extractors, shared app state, and explicit error-to-response mapping
- Require authentication for every read/write endpoint that touches multisig state
- Use nonce + expiry bounded sessions and bind each session to exactly one multisig authority
- Verify signer authorization against the canonical signer set derived from current onchain ASM state
- Ensure non-signers cannot infer pending proposal existence through status codes, timing, or payload differences

## Data and Lifecycle

- Model proposal identity as deterministic and idempotent (`ActionId = hash(MultisigAction, SeqNo)`)
- Keep sequence numbers as `u64` and preserve protocol behavior that allows skipped sequence values
- Enforce duplicate prevention for identical `(MultisigAction, SeqNo)` submissions
- Track explicit lifecycle states required by product flows (Pending, Approved, Enacted, Canceled, Expired, and payout-specific states)
- Preserve offline survivability: backend downtime must not block signers from manually aggregating signatures and broadcasting transactions

## Testing and Reliability

- Add unit tests for authority isolation, duplicate prevention, and lifecycle transitions
- Add integration tests for auth/session flows and multisig-scoped access control
- Prefer deterministic serialization/hash fixtures for protocol payload compatibility checks
- Keep observability structured (request IDs, authority scope, action IDs) without logging sensitive signing material
